.TH COAL 1 "October 2026" "coal" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
coal \- compile a directory of YAML files into one validated YAML configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coal
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR [NAME=]VALUE ]...
.RB [ \-p
.IR PATH=VALUE ]...
.RI [ PRINT_PATH ]
.br
.B coal
.B \-a
.I ANALYZE_PATH
.br
.B coal
.RB { \-h | \-V }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B coal
(Coalescence) reads the directory it is run in and builds one YAML
configuration out of it.
The directory is the configuration: a folder is a level of nesting and a
file is a field, so
.I services/api.yaml
becomes
.BR services.api .
Shared settings are written once in
.I _each
files, reusable shapes live in
.I _definitions
directories, and any value may be a Jinja template that reads any other
part of the configuration.
.PP
Directives starting with
.B $
(such as
.BR $include ,
.BR $include_optional ,
.BR $inherit ,
.BR $ignore ,
.B $append
and
.BR $extend )
are expanded while the tree is built.
A schema in
.I _schema/
describes what is valid, and may itself use Jinja to take allowed values
from the configuration.
.PP
When the directory holds a
.IR _structure.yaml ,
it describes several configurations at once (for example one per
environment and node); the
.B \-\-select
option picks one of them.
.PP
The compiled configuration is written to standard output.
Diagnostics are written to standard error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-validate
Check the configuration against the schema instead of printing it.
With a
.I _structure.yaml
and no selection, every configuration the structure describes is built
and checked, and each finding is reported once, in the file and on the
line where it is written.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-select " " \fI[NAME=]VALUE\fR
Build one configuration out of the many the tree describes, as declared
in
.IR _structure.yaml .
Give the option once per selector.
The value is any right\-aligned part of the selector's path, so
.B "\-s environments.production.nodes.cop01"
can be used when the short name
.B cop01
would match more than one place.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-param " " \fIPATH=VALUE\fR
Pin a value at a dotted path that the configuration cannot override.
The value is seeded into the tree before the build, so it can drive
includes, layers and selections.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-analyze " " \fIANALYZE_PATH\fR
Dump the internal node properties of one field (key, value, resolved
schema type, origin file, allowed values and source span), for example
.BR "coal \-a processes.cms" .
.TP
.I PRINT_PATH
Print only this part of the compiled configuration, for example
.BR "coal environments.production" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I _schema/
Schema definitions the configuration is validated against.
.TP
.I _structure.yaml
Declares the selectors and layers that turn one tree into many
configurations.
.TP
.I _definitions/
Named shapes that
.B $include
can splice into a field.
.TP
.IR _each.yaml ", " _each/
Settings applied to every sibling at that level.
.TP
.I _self.yaml
The fields of the folder it sits in.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B coal
exits with status 0 when the build (or validation) succeeds, and with a
non\-zero status when the configuration cannot be built or validation
reports problems.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build the configuration in the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B coal > config.yaml
.RE
.PP
Check every configuration a
.I _structure.yaml
describes:
.PP
.RS
.B coal \-\-validate
.RE
.PP
Build the staging configuration for node cop01 and print only its
processes:
.PP
.RS
.B coal \-s staging \-s cop01 processes
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR coal\-lsp (1)
.PP
The full language reference is in
.I /usr/share/doc/coal/README.md.gz
and at
.UR https://github.com/coal\-config/coal
.UE .
